## 1. UTF-8 stress-test corpus (Markus Kuhn), frozen 2026-08-08

`src/connection.zig`'s `kuhn_utf8_vectors` transcribes a representative subset
of Markus Kuhn's UTF-8 decoder capability-and-stress test, reached via
`autobahntestsuite`'s `case/case6_x_x.py`, which embeds it verbatim with an
explicit citation to its origin:

  UTF-8 decoder capability and stress test
  Markus Kuhn <http://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~mgk25/> — 2015-08-28
  http://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~mgk25/ucs/examples/UTF-8-test.txt
  "You may use this file for any purpose."

What is reproduced is conformance DATA — byte sequences and whether each is
well-formed UTF-8 — not code. See SPEC.md "External-anchor investigation" for
why the same suite's §7.9 close-code cases were deliberately NOT frozen.


## 2. Foreign-peer verdict corpus, frozen 2026-08-09

`src/connection.zig`'s `foreign_peer_corpus` records what three independently
authored WebSocket implementations DID when 54 byte strings were put in front
of them:

  * python-websockets 15.0.1 — BSD-3-Clause
    Copyright (c) Aymeric Augustin and contributors
    https://github.com/python-websockets/websockets
  * github.com/coder/websocket v1.8.15 — ISC
    Copyright (c) 2023 Anmol Sethi
  * github.com/gorilla/websocket v1.5.3 — BSD-2-Clause
    Copyright (c) 2023 The Gorilla WebSocket Authors

Licence reasoning, stated explicitly because the brief that produced this
corpus requires it:

  * All three licences are permissive (BSD-3-Clause / ISC / BSD-2-Clause) and
    impose no condition MIT does not already impose. Even a full copy would
    have been unproblematic; none was made.
  * Nothing is copied, translated, or redistributed from any of them. Not one
    line of their source appears here, and no conclusion was drawn by reading
    their source — the corpus is the OBSERVED OUTPUT of running them.
  * The frozen `wire` fields are byte strings this repository composed itself
    (RFC 6455 §5 frames, masked with the §5.7 mask key). The frozen `peers`
    fields are numeric close codes and accept/reject outcomes those three
    programs emitted on a socket in response. Observed numeric behaviour of a
    public wire specification is not a copyrightable expression of theirs.
  * That statement is true of what was actually done: the capture scripts live
    outside the repository in `~/.cache/zig-libs-websocket/` and read no
    upstream source file; the Go harness only dials a loopback socket and
    records the bytes that come back.

The three implementations are build- and test-time absent. These tests run
offline, never skip, and are unaffected by deleting all three.
